{"context":"A platform product manages behavior across multiple subsystems — release lifecycle, artifact packaging, pipeline execution, state management, CLI interaction — each with its own configuration needs. Without a unified approach, each subsystem naturally develops its own configuration mechanism.\n\nThis fragmentation produces well-known problems:\n\n**Inconsistent discovery.** Each subsystem defines its own configuration sources. Some read files, some read environment variables, some use compiled defaults. Users must learn multiple configuration mechanisms for a single product.\n\n**Unpredictable precedence.** When configuration values conflict, there is no consistent rule for which value wins. One subsystem may let environment variables override files; another may do the opposite. The same setting Source: ADR-005-configuration-system.md Date:2026-07-19 Status:Accepted.","decision":"Anvil adopts a unified Configuration System.\n\nThe Configuration System is a product-wide architectural service. Every subsystem consumes configuration through the same conceptual model.\n\nNo subsystem owns its own independent configuration mechanism. Subsystems MAY define their own configuration schema — which configuration values they require — but they MUST NOT define their own mechanism for discovery, loading, resolution, or validation.\n\nThe unified model means:\n\n- Configuration discovery follows a single, predictable strategy\n- Configuration resolution follows a single, documented precedence order\n- Configuration validation follows a single, canonical schema\n- Configuration provision follows a single, immutable access model\n\nA subsystem that needs a configuration value declares what it ","consequences":"### 9.1 Positive Consequences\n\n**Predictability.** Every consumer resolves configuration the same way. There is no ambiguity about which value will be used for any given key.\n\n**Consistency.** Users interact with a single configuration model regardless of which subsystem they are configuring. The discovery, resolution, and validation mechanisms are uniform.\n\n**Simpler extensions.** Framework adapters extend configuration through a documented extension mechanism. They do not need to invent their own configuration approach.\n\n**Unified developer experience.** Developers working with Anvil learn one configuration model, not one per subsystem. This reduces the learning curve and the risk of misconfiguration.\n\n**Reduced configuration drift.** Because all configuration flows through a single syst","alternativesConsidered":"Considered\n\n### 10.1 Independent Configuration Per Subsystem\n\n**Description:** Each subsystem — Release Engine, Artifact System, Pipeline Orchestrator — owns its own configuration mechanism. Subsystems independently decide how configuration is discovered, loaded, validated, and provided.\n\n**Rejected because:** This approach guarantees fragmentation. Each subsystem would develop its own conventions for discovery, precedence, validation, and defaults. Users would need to learn multiple configurati"}